    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d411">

                <pb facs="0498" n="1332" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1332"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 659.119/367</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Laughlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-10T10:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 10, 1918, 10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 10.02 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>219. War Trade Board from Doctor Taylor:</p>
                <p>No. 976. If Danes accept your shipping proposal there remains still the matter of
                    exports to Germany. At last meeting with me in <pb facs="0499" n="1333"
                        xml:id="pg1333"/> Washington Danes advanced new proposals for exports to
                    Germany that could not be regarded as related to previous negotiations.
                    Acceptance by them of our shipping proposal does not carry with it acceptance by
                    us of their new proposal of export of fish and agricultural products. This new
                    proposal they stated had been imposed on them in order to secure immunity from
                    new enemy prize court rule. These proposals French and British regard as
                    impossible just as we did. I will take up with Allies acceptation of export
                    clause along line of old agreement and report.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Laughlin</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
